From: mscharged-port
Subject: Key the AI question cache the same way on every host
Upstream-base: 9046e67aa2077a7eb4ed6e28c9df3c1d3b368f84
Classification: native compiler/ABI compatibility

The AI scripts cache the answer to a question for the rest of the step.
FuzzyTryCachedQuestion looks it up by a key, the question's instruction
offset plus its argument's hash, and on a miss leaves the key in the action
queue entry's int mQuestionHash; EndActionQueue then stores the answer under
that key. On Linux unsigned long has 64 bits and the int is sign-extended
back into it, so a key with its top bit set was stored as
0xffffffff........ and never found: Linux asked those questions again where
Windows and the Wii took the cached answer. The AI scripts of a Linux and a
Windows machine ran other branches from the first second of a match, and
the match fell out of sync once their choices reached the simulation (a
goalie checking for a deke attack one step earlier). Natively the stored key
is the 32-bit word the int holds.

The key also hashed a player, team, ball or game argument as its address,
which each host places differently, so two questions sharing a key (and an
answer) on one machine did not on the other. Natively such an argument
stands in as a fixed Wii-like address of its own (players by their slot in
g_pCharacters, teams by index, the ball and the game), spaced as far apart
as the objects are on the Wii, and the key is a 32-bit word. Every host then
builds the same keys. The Wii build is unchanged.

diff --git a/src/Game/AI/FuzzyRuntimeBase.cpp b/src/Game/AI/FuzzyRuntimeBase.cpp
index f0411aa..0f9868a 100644
